{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Turing Machine.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Turing Machine.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"turing machine turing machine","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Turing Machine.md","fileName":"Turing Machine.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Turing Machine","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Algorithm Foundations]] · [[Wartime Computing and Early Electronic Machines]] · [[Turing Machines and Computability]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Algorithm Foundations","Wartime Computing and Early Electronic Machines","Turing Machines and Computability","Algorithm","Turing-Decidable Language","Turing-Acceptable Language","algorithms.epub","computing.epub","FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Turing Machine.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Turing Machine.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"turing machine turing machine","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Turing Machine.md","fileName":"Turing Machine.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Turing Machine","heading":"Turing Machine","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A Turing machine is an idealized computational machine consisting of an unbounded tape divided into cells, a movable read-write head, a finite set of states, and an instruction table. Each instruction uses the current state and scanned symbol to choose a new state, write a symbol, and move the head one cell left or right.\n\nIts operations are deliberately elementary, yet a properly constructed Turing machine can implement any [[Algorithm]]. This makes it a formal model for reasoning about what computation can accomplish, independent of the speed or physical construction of a particular computer.\n\nFormally, its transition function maps the 